Shorten the bootstrap bodies that spell Dracon, anchors qualified, through canonical scoped generation

Dev accepted, on 2026-10-21, "shortening the bootstrap files' bodies against the canonical identity
ontology and HostBindings, with nominal anchors kept qualified, the Scala regenerated through the
scoped generator with selective imports, DracoGenTest made scoped, starting with the definitions
that spell the Dracon path."

The seventeen main definitions that spelled draco.format.dracon.Dracon — the TypeElement family's
eleven kinds, TypeName, TypeParameter, DracoAspect, DomainAspectOf, DracoAspectOf, CirceCodecs — are
swept: their JSON bodies are the compact form against the canonical identity ontology and the
ScalaTarget's HostBindings, their drake is re-emitted, their anchors (typeName, domain pointer,
derivation records) stay qualified, so the companions, the engine's parent chains and the ontology's
edges read them unscoped as before. The compact form itself now keeps every nominal anchor in full.

Scoped generation had to change to carry this. The engine reads a definition under its own
conventions — a bare name is looked up in the file's own package for codec eligibility, realized
through its host import table, or qualified — and several decisions key on those spellings (the
codec's optional-field elision, the payload check, the actor-minting factory). Resolving a definition
in full before generating broke them. The scoped generator therefore spells a definition
CANONICALLY: the compact form, re-qualified only where the engine cannot read a bare name
(DracoGenerator.readable: a host primitive; a table name the host realizes — not a draco type that
shares its simple name, Decoder and Encoder, and not in the file's own package, where the standard
circe import outranks a member; an own-package name no sibling package or host class shadows,
draco.Generated being the hand-written case), generates it, and shortens exactly those identities
back by selective import where Scala's own scoping allows (an exact import, a wildcard, the own
package) and never into a host-shadowed name. The output no longer depends on how an author spelled
a resolvable name, so a resolved definition and its compact form generate identically. Every
definition the engine loads during a scoped generation is spelled the same way, through a
thread-local scope in effect; an unknown name in a loaded definition leaves it as loaded. The
actor-minting factory accepts draco.ActorType by identity.

Because the engine now canonicalizes, every definition-backed Scala file was regenerated through the
scoped generator: 35 changed — 25 under src/main/scala and the ten staged dreams and service files —
gaining selective imports for cross-package names (`import draco.format.dracon.Dracon`,
`import draco.drake.Presence`, `import org.evrete.api.ActivationMode`) and short spellings; the rest
were byte-identical. DracoGenTest generates every type through the canonical scope, as do
GenerateAndCompileTest, GeneratorDefinitionToSourceTest, TypeDefinitionTest, DreamsTest,
InitializationTest, EditorTest and ServiceTest, all through the new CanonicalScope test helper (the
canonical declarations plus HostBindings.vocabulary, supplied explicitly); the unscoped engine
cannot read a swept body's referrers correctly, which these suites showed at their first run.
NameResolutionTest now expects a nominal anchor to stay qualified in the compact surface.

Probe on the compiled engine over a resource overlay: 29 suites, 770 passed, zero failed or aborted,
every headline unchanged, pending mods actors 0; the whole main and mods tree compiles against the
regenerated classes (the one error outside sbt's build, scripts/inspect-type.scala, predates this
change). No test was added; no corpus type changed. The rest of the corpus's bodies follow in a later
sweep.

Dev's first full gate failed the three primes rules in DracoGenTest (842 of 845): PrimesRulesTest
writes its generation into src/main/scala, unscoped, and overwrote the regenerated files before the
gate compared them. It now generates through the canonical scope. The regenerated rules had also
gained `import java.lang.Integer` and `import java.lang.System`, because HostBindings gave those
names java.lang identities while the Scala target reads them bare; Integer, System and Enumeration
are now bound bare, as Seq and Map are, so the primes rules regenerate byte-identically to before.

Gate: Dev's third full sbt run on 2026-10-21, pasted (the second failed to compile on an unqualified
helper reference in the primes test): 845 tests / 59 suites, zero failed, canceled, ignored or pending
tests, zero aborted suites; every report-only headline unchanged; pending mods actors 0 in the file-only
log. Committed and pushed by Dev.

